{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>כרכב המזבח.</b> Surrounding; anything that encircles round in a circle is called a כַּרְכֹּב, as we learned in the Mishnah: 'With what may they slaughter? These are the rough forms of wooden vessels — anything destined to be smoothed and וּלְכַרְכֵּב.' And it is like what one does — making rounded grooves in the boards of the sides of chests and wooden benches; so too for the altar he made a groove around it, and its width was a cubit on its side for ornament. And it was at the top of the three cubits of its height, according to the one who says its height was twice its length [i.e., ten cubits]. If so, how do I fulfill ״וְשָׁלֹשׁ אַמּוֹת קוֹמָתוֹ״? From the lip of the surround and upward. But a surround for the priests to walk upon — the altar of bronze did not have, except at its top, on the inside of its horns. And so we learned in Zevaḥim: What is the כַּרְכֹּב? Between horn and horn; and it was a cubit wide, and on the inside of them a cubit for walking of the priests' feet — these two cubits are called כַּרְכֹּב. But we examined there: is it not written ״תַּחַת כַּרְכֹּב הַמִּזְבֵּחַ מִלְּמַטָּה״? We learned that the כַּרְכֹּב is on its side and the covering of the mesh was beneath it. And the resolver resolved: there were two — one for ornament and one for the priests, that they not slip; this one on the side was for ornament, and beneath it they clothed it with the mesh, and its width reached to half the altitude of the altar; and it was a sign for half its height, to distinguish between the upper bloods and the lower bloods. And corresponding to it they made for the altar of the House of Eternity a replica of the חוּט הַסִּקְרָא thread in its middle. And as for the ramp by which they would ascend — even though it is not stated in this matter, we have already heard in the section ״מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי״ and ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְּמַעֲלוֹת״ — do not make steps in its ramp, but a smooth ramp; we learned that it had a ramp. Thus we learned in the Mekhilta. And ״מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה״ is the altar of bronze, for they would fill its hollow with earth in the place of their encampment. And the ramp was on the south of the altar, separated from the altar by a hair's-breadth thread, and its feet reached to a cubit adjacent to the hangings of the courtyard on the south, according to the one who says its height was ten cubits; and according to the one who says the matter as written — ״שְׁלֹשׁ אַמּוֹת קוֹמָתוֹ״ — the length of the ramp was only ten cubits. Thus I found in the Mishnah of Forty-Nine Measures. And that it was separated from the altar by the width of the thread — in tractate Zevaḥim (62b) they derived it from Scripture."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "Rashi's comment is unusually long and layered, moving from lexicography through mishnaic sources to a structural dispute about the altar's dimensions; I have preserved every clause in sequence without compression. The unresolved quotation וְהַכְתִיב תַּחַת כַּרְכֹּב... מִלְּמָטָה is rendered as Rashi's own hyper-literal objection, which the 'resolver' then answers."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:55:06.183681084Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}